 ## Background
-The method originated with the rise of programmable graphics hardware in the early 2000s, which allowed for the offloading of volume rendering computations to the GPU [S2, unofficial]. Key developments include the use of 3D textures for volume storage and the implementation of transfer functions as 1D textures [S1][S2, unofficial]. The approach was popularized by techniques such as those described in "GPU Gems" Chapter 39 and various academic papers on hardware-accelerated volume rendering [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical imaging, scientific visualization, and real-time interactive applications where performance is critical [S2, unofficial].
+The technique originated with the advent of 3D texture hardware in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Early implementations, such as those by Krüger and Westermann (2003), utilized proxy geometry to leverage the graphics pipeline's texture sampling capabilities [S2, unofficial]. The "Object-Aligned Slicing" variant was popularized by Lefohn et al. (2004) and Kniss et al. (2002b), who addressed memory and sampling issues by replicating data along principal axes [S1]. View-aligned slicing was introduced to mitigate the sampling distance variations inherent in object-aligned approaches [S2, unofficial]. The method has been extended to Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data by Kähler and Hege (2001/2002) and Kähler et al. (2002), who applied it to astrophysical datasets like 'The First Star of the Universe' [S3, unofficial]. More recently, it has been adapted for indirect illumination via Layered Polygonal Area Lights (LPALs) using Ray-to-Gravity-Center slicing [S4].
 
